; TEST: expect-exit=0 c-emit-exit=4
;
; THE ONE FIXTURE WHERE THE TWO BACKENDS LEGITIMATELY DISAGREE, and the
; reason `c-emit-exit=` exists at all (docs/design/c-backend.md D6 step 4).
;
; `retain`/`release` are two of the reference's 37 lowerings: emit.inc's
; `__bfa_emit_arc` calls the prelude's ARC routines over an object header the
; runtime owns. The C backend in LIBRARY MODE has no runtime and no object
; header -- D1 withholds both deliberately, because the first consumer is a
; freestanding N64 library that can host neither -- so it refuses the pair BY
; NAME rather than emitting something that would compile and be wrong:
;
;   bfc: emitter: retain/release: no object header exists in library mode
;
; So this fixture's directive says `emit-exit=0` (the reference lowers it)
; and `c-emit-exit=4` (the C emitter refuses it), and the differential phase
; reports the difference as INTENDED rather than as a failure. Twenty-one of
; the other twenty-two refusals need no such key: the C backend refuses them
; because the REFERENCE refuses them, so parity is the default and the
; default is right.
;
; THE ARC LIVES IN A FUNCTION NOTHING CALLS, and that is deliberate. The
; reference's half must ASSEMBLE AND RUN to be worth anything -- a fixture
; checked only as far as emission would not notice the day the reference's
; ARC lowering stopped assembling. But EXECUTING `retain` needs a `refc`
; pointing at a real object header, and `initium`'s only caller is the
; prelude's entry stub, which passes one argument. So `@arca` carries the
; pair and is never reached: the reference emits it, the assembler accepts
; it, the program runs `@initium` alone and exits 0, and the C emitter still
; meets `retain` while walking the module and still refuses.
;
; The operand is `refc` and not `ptr` because verifier rule 6 admits only
; `ref`/`refc` for the ARC pair -- a `slot`'s `ptr` fails the VERIFIER (exit
; 5) before either emitter is reached, and a fixture that never reaches the
; emitter proves nothing about what the emitter does. Found by running it.
;
; Mutation (run): __bfc_refuse's `.arc` arm deleted so `retain` falls through
; to `.other` -> still exit 4, but the message names no opcode; the arm
; pointed at `.divrem` -> exit 4 with a message naming an opcode that is not
; here. Neither is caught by an exit status alone, which is why the refusal
; text is printed into the run log and read there.

functio @arca (refc) -> void {
b0:
%0 = param refc 0
retain %0
release %0
ret
}
functio @initium (ptr) -> u8 {
b0:
%0 = param ptr 0
%1 = iconst u8 0
ret %1
}
